=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r13
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The FRR `ospfd` daemon on r13 is not running, and it has been removed from `watchfrr`'s supervision list, so it will not be auto-restarted.

**Mechanism:** r13 has four loopback interfaces with real prefixes (113.1.1.1/24, 113.1.2.1/24, 113.1.3.1/24, 113.1.4.1/24) and an uplink to r11 (103.1.1.2/8 on eth0, FF segment). Without ospfd, r13 generates no router-LSAs, so none of its 113.1.x.x/24 loopback networks are flooded into area 0.0.0.3 or beyond. Those prefixes are completely dark to every other router in the domain.

**Localization:** r13, all interfaces. The `watchfrr` daemon list reads `zebra mgmtd eigrpd staticd` — `ospfd` has been dropped from `/etc/frr/daemons`, so neither watchfrr nor systemd will restart it. The control socket (`ospfd_vty`) is absent, confirmed by both the ospf_snapshot helper and a direct `show ip ospf neighbor` call returning "ospfd is not running."

**Clarification on NOC hypothesis:** R7's ospfd is fully operational (1 Full adjacency to R6, 59 OSPF routes, self-originating LSAs for 17.1.x.x/32, 67.0.0.0/8, and 70.0.0.0/8). R1 has all R7-region routes correctly. The NOC was searching for /24 summaries whereas OSPF advertises loopbacks as /32 host routes — no routes were actually missing at R1.

**Root cause:** ospfd was killed and removed from the FRR daemon configuration on r13, silently withdrawing the 113.1.x.x/24 loopback prefixes from the entire OSPF domain.

**Fix:** Re-enable ospfd on r13 by adding `ospfd=yes` to `/etc/frr/daemons`, restoring the OSPF `network` statements for the 113.x.x.x and 103.x.x.x prefixes, and restarting FRR (`service frr restart`).
=== END ===